The way I handled this in [my library](https://github.com/weichch/ExceptionMapper/blob/master/src/libraries/RabbitSharp.ExceptionMapper.AspNetCore/EndpointExceptionMappingMiddleware.cs#L165) is creating a new feature which contains two properties:

```
interface IExceptionHandlerEndpointFeature
{
    Endpoint? Endpoint { get; set; }
    RouteValueDictionary? RouteValues { get; set; }
}
```

I always captured the context and set the feature to `HttpContext`. Not sure if that's considered a breaking change, but if so, we could have a boolean value in `ExceptionHandlerOptions` to specify whether the capture is needed.

@Tratcher We could introduce a new `IExceptionHandlerEndpointFeature` which hold `Endpoint` and `RouteValueDictionary`
```
public interface IExceptionHandlerEndpointFeature
{
    Endpoint? Endpoint { get; set; }
    RouteValueDictionary? RouteValues { get; set; }
}
```
and set the feature before `ClearHttpContext` and also remove `IRouteValuesFeature` from features like you suggested or set the `IRouteValuesFeature.RouteValues` to null. The implementation would create a new dictionary if it's null.
https://github.com/dotnet/aspnetcore/blob/52eff90fbcfca39b7eb58baad597df6a99a542b0/src/Http/Http/src/Features/RouteValuesFeature.cs#L20-L30
